# THROWAWAY TEST FIXTURE — NOT A REAL myPOS CERTIFICATE, NOT A SECRET.
#
# Self-signed X.509 certificate generated for tests/unit/mypos.service.test.ts
# (BC-MYPOS-003-FOLLOWUP-1, item 4) so the `BEGIN CERTIFICATE` branch of
# `MyPOSService.resolveVerificationKey()` is actually exercised by a unit
# test. Node's `crypto` module alone cannot self-sign an X.509 certificate
# (there is no `crypto.createCertificate`), which is why this fixture is
# checked in rather than generated in-process the way the rest of this test
# file generates its RSA keypairs via `crypto.generateKeyPairSync`.
#
# Generated with:
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 \
#     -keyout mypos-test-self-signed-key.pem \
#     -out mypos-test-self-signed-cert.pem \
#     -days 36500 -nodes -sha256 \
#     -subj "/CN=mypos-test-fixture.invalid/O=BoomCard test fixture - not a real myPOS certificate"
#
# SHA-256 fingerprint (`openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256 -in
# mypos-test-self-signed-cert.pem`):
#   6A:4F:F9:F2:12:D1:AD:DE:F6:81:EB:0E:55:AC:8E:5D:CF:DB:6F:C2:48:D0:29:08:C4:EB:EB:2E:3F:F3:58:45
#
# Paired private key: mypos-test-self-signed-key.pem (same directory). Both
# files are throwaway key material with no relationship to any real myPOS
# store, key, or credential — same class as the ephemeral
# `crypto.generateKeyPairSync` keypairs generated elsewhere in this test
# file, just pre-generated because the certificate wrapper needs `openssl`.
